  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nThe stage has been set for another dangerously hot and humid \nafternoon and evening in Southern California. Several locations \nin the coastal basin astonishingly did not drop below 80 degrees \nthis morning. Strong upper ridging is peaking today and tonight \nwith 500 mb heights around 595-596 mb. Also, surface analysis \nreveals a thermal trough sitting near or just offshore as of this \nmorning, which will hinder the attempt of the seabreeze to cool \nthe coastal basin. Temperatures this afternoon are currently  \nranging from 100-110 for inland areas and generally in the 90s \nnear the coast. The combination of heat and humidity have pushed \nheat indices above 100 even near the coastline this afternoon, \nproducing dangerous conditions for those without air-conditioning.\nIn fact, heat indices are locally exceeding 115 in the western \nvalleys of San Diego and Orange Counties. Overall, major to \nextreme HeatRisk is occurring or imminent in the coastal basin \ntoday and will last well into the evening hours. Major HeatRisk is\nalso expected in the Coachella Valley this afternoon where \ntemperatures will peak near 105-110. Moderate HeatRisk elsewhere, \nexcept for mountain locations above 6000 ft, where it should be a \nrather pleasant next few days.\n\nSpeaking of the mountains, isolated to scattered thunderstorms are \nexpected again this afternoon. Storm activity exceeded expectations \nyesterday, so we are hedging on the side of more activity again \ntoday. There is less instability and greater capping today as \nindicated by the 12z KNKX sounding, but PWATs remain very high near \n1.8 inches. Isolated rainfall rates near 0.5 in/hr will likely occur \ntoday along with potential for gusty winds with any storm that \ndevelops. Southeasterly flow will permit a slight chance (10-20%) \nfor a storm or two to drift into the eastern valleys. For Thursday, \ninstability will decrease yet again. Therefore, expecting only \nisolated showers over ridgetops at this time.\n\nA slow cooling trend begins on Thursday as the upper ridge begins to \nweaken. However, this humid, tropical airmass will remain in place \nthrough Friday. Therefore, areas of major HeatRisk will continue \nthrough Thursday for the coastal basin and through Friday for the \nlow deserts. By Saturday, an upper trough to the northwest will \nbegin to push the weakening ridge out of the region bringing more \nrelief from the heat and humidity. High temperatures will fall 5 to \n10 degrees below normal across the region from Sunday into early \nnext week.\n\nMorning marine layer clouds and fog will likely begin to reform \nover coastal waters as moderate onshore flow returns from Friday \nonward. There is a chance for fog Saturday morning for coastal \nareas and western valleys. Thereafter, the marine layer should \ndeepen further into early next week with low clouds filling most \nof the coastal basin each morning. Additionally, breezy conditions\nare expected for outer coastal waters, mountains, and deserts \nover the weekend. By late next week model solutions diverge, but \nthe mean of the guidance tilts toward high pressure and monsoonal \nmoisture building back into the region from the east.",
